{{Use British English|date=February 2016}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=March 2024}}

[[File:Celtic manager Jock Stein in 1971.jpg|thumb|220px|alt=Photograph taken of Jock Stein in the early 1970s|[[Jock Stein]] is considered to be Celtic's greatest manager.]]

[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic Football Club]] is a Scottish association football club based in [[Glasgow]]. The club was founded in 1887 and played their first match in May 1888.<ref name="CFCBriefHistory" /> Celtic have won the [[Scottish football league system|Scottish League Championship]] on 56 occasions, the [[Scottish Cup]] 42 times and the [[Scottish League Cup]] 22 times.<ref>{{cite web|title=About</ref>

As of the 2025–26 season, Celtic have had 20 different full-time managers. [[Willie Maley]], the club's first manager, is the longest to have served in the post, having managed the club from 1897 to 1940. The 30 major honours Maley won during his tenure are the most a manager has achieved at Celtic. 

Under Jock Stein, Celtic enjoyed their greatest period of sustained success. Stein assumed management in 1965 and over the next decade transformed the club into a dominant force domestically and a major contender in Europe. Between 1966–67 and 1974–75, Celtic won nine consecutive Scottish league championships. Under his leadership the club also won multiple Scottish Cups and League Cups. Most notably, in 1967 Stein guided Celtic — later dubbed the “Lisbon Lions” — to victory in the European Cup, making them the first British club ever to win Europe’s premier club competition. That triumph remains Celtic’s only European Cup win to date and stands as the pinnacle of the club’s mid-20th century achievements.<ref>{{cite web |title=1967: When Celtic made history |publisher=UEFA.com |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0221-0f8a1a89c336-84dfe037d80a-1000--1967-when-celtic-made-history/ |access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Kelly |first=John |title=Celtic: The Complete Record |publisher=DB Publishing |year=2005 |isbn=1903573620}}</ref>

Martin O'Neill is widely regarded as the most influential Celtic manager since Jock Stein.<ref name="BBCProfile">{{cite news |title=Martin O'Neill: Celtic Years |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/ |work=BBC Sport |access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref> Appointed in June 2000, he revived a club that had endured a period of domestic under-achievement and quickly restored Celtic to domestic dominance. In his first season he guided the team to a domestic treble, the club’s first since the Stein era in the late 1960s.<ref name="Treble2001">{{cite news |title=Celtic complete historic treble |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/ |work=The Guardian |date=27 May 2001 |access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref> That season also featured a 6–2 victory over Rangers — at the time the largest margin of victory over their great rivals in more than four decades.<ref name="ONeillRangers2000">{{cite web |title=Celtic 6–2 Rangers (2000): Old Firm classic |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/2000-08-27-celtic-6-2-rangers-premier-league/ |access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref> Celtic then retained the league title in 2001–02, the first time the club had done so since 1982.<ref name="League2002">{{cite web |title=Scottish Premier League 2001–02 |url=https://www.scottishfootballarchive.co.uk/spl-2001-02 |publisher=Scottish Football Archive |access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref> Most significantly, O’Neill also took Celtic to the 2003 UEFA Cup Final — their first European final since 1970 — re-establishing the club’s competitiveness on the continental stage.<ref name="UEFA2003">{{cite web |title=2003 UEFA Cup Final Report |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/history/seasons/2003/ |publisher=UEFA |access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref> His win rate of 75.5% is the highest of any manager in the club's history.<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic Manager Records |url=https://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/manager_records.php |publisher=fitba stats |access-date=31 January 2021}}</ref> O'Neill returned to Celtic in 2025 at the age of 73 during a crisis hit season. He managed Celtic to a Scottish Premier League and Scottish Cup double, further cementing his legendary status at the football club.<ref>{{cite news |last=McLauchlin |first=Brian |title=Celtic complete domestic double as O'Neill wins fourth Scottish Cup |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/celtic-dunfermline-result-scottish-cup-martin-oneill-b2982536.html |work=The Independent |date=23 May 2026 |access-date=23 May 2026}}</ref>

This chronological list comprises all those who have held the position of [[Manager (association football)|manager]] of the first team of Celtic since its foundation in 1887. Each manager's entry includes the dates of tenure and the club's overall competitive record in terms of matches won, drawn and lost, and of major honours won.  [[Caretaker manager]]s are also included.

==Managerial history==
===1888–1965===
For the first few years following its formation, the administrative and team selection duties at Celtic were performed by a committee. [[Willie Maley]] had played for the team since their inaugural match in May 1888, and on his retirement at the age of 29 was appointed secretary-manager in April 1897, effectively becoming the team's first ever manager.<ref name="celticfc" /><ref>{{cite web|title=If You Know The History: 2nd April: Willie Maley died 1958|url=https://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2012/04/02/if-you-know-the-history-2nd-april-willie-maley-died-1958/|website=Jim Craig Celtic Supporter's Club|access-date=18 September 2015|date=2 April 2012|archive-date=4 February 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160204153422/https://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2012/04/02/if-you-know-the-history-2nd-april-willie-maley-died-1958/|url-status=live}}</ref> His role was quite different to the modern style of [[Manager (association football)|manager]] or [[head coach]]; he never worked with his players in training and only watched games from the director's box. He did not perform team talks or speak with the players at half time or immediately post-match.<ref>{{cite news |first=Tom |last=Sweetman |title=Longest serving managers in football |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/incoming/longest-serving-managers-in-football-6257315.html |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=4 November 2011 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127181316/https://www.independent.co.uk/incoming/longest-serving-managers-in-football-6257315.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Having been a club that initially relied on buying in experienced players, Maley instead concentrated on developing young talent.<ref name=Bathgate>{{cite news |last=Bathgate |first=Stuart |title=Celtic fans get chance to own medal won by club legend Willie Maley |url=https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/teams/celtic/celtic-fans-get-chance-to-own-medal-won-by-club-legend-willie-maley-1-2549113 |newspaper=The Scotsman |location=Edinburgh |date=27 September 2012 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127183809/https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/teams/celtic/celtic-fans-get-chance-to-own-medal-won-by-club-legend-willie-maley-1-2549113 |url-status=live }}</ref> This proved a success as Celtic won six consecutive league titles in the first decade of the 20th century.<ref name="hof">{{cite web|title=Willie Maley|url=http://scottishfootballhalloffame.co.uk/willie-maley-1868-1958/|website=Scottish Football Hall of Fame|access-date=28 August 2015|archive-date=10 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910094732/http://scottishfootballhalloffame.co.uk/willie-maley-1868-1958/|url-status=live}}</ref> In 1907 the team also became the first Scottish side win a league and [[Scottish Cup|cup]] [[Double (association football)|double]].<ref name="Campbell & Woods 1987 73">{{cite book|last1=Campbell|last2=Woods|first1=Tom|first2=Pat|title=The Glory & The Dream|publisher=Grafton Books|isbn=0-586-20005-3|date=1987|page=73}}</ref> The next decade saw Maley lead Celtic to a further four successive league titles between 1914 and 1917,<ref name="CFCBriefHistory">{{cite news |url=http://www.celticfc.net/pages/history |title=Brief History |publisher=Celtic F.C. |access-date=27 August 2015 |archive-date=10 May 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160510143304/http://www.celticfc.net/pages/history |url-status=live }}</ref> during which time they went on an unbeaten run for 62 games from 13 November 1915 until 21 April 1917.<ref name="unb">{{cite web|title=Celtic FC's series of 62 matches unbeaten in Division One|url=https://www.rsssf.org/miscellaneous/unb-celtic-62.html|publisher=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation|access-date=16 October 2013|first=Alan|last=Brown|archive-date=14 March 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130314065317/http://rsssf.com/miscellaneous/unb-celtic-62.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Longest unbeaten runs in European league football |url=http://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/news/newsid=1745954.html |publisher=UEFA |date=13 December 2017 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=14 June 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170614055608/http://www.uefa.com/memberassociations/news/newsid=1745954.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> This remained a British record for an unbeaten run in professional football until it was surpassed by [[Brendan Rodgers]]' Celtic over 100 years later.<ref name="unb" /> Towards the end of his reign, Maley led Celtic to further League titles in 1936 and 1938 and the Scottish Cup in 1937.<ref name="hof" /> He remained in his post for almost 43 years, the longest serving manager in the club's history, and guided the team to 30 major trophies.<ref name="celticfc" /><ref name=Bathgate/><ref name="hof" />

[[Jimmy McStay]], who made over 400 appearances for Celtic as a player and captained the side, was appointed manager in 1940. His tenure was during [[World War II]], a period when Scottish football suffered huge disruption. He also had to contend with increased boardroom interference, which he never managed to quell. The result was stagnation with little tangible success.<ref name="celticfc" /> In the summer of 1945, McStay reluctantly resigned at the behest of the board<ref>{{cite book|title=Celtic – A Century with Honour|last=Wilson|first=Brian|date=1988|publisher=Willow Books|isbn=0-00-218230-0|page=82}}</ref> and was succeeded by another celebrated former player, [[Jimmy McGrory]].<ref name="celticfc" /> The early years of his tenure were very poor, and in 1948 the club only narrowly avoided relegation.<ref name="wilson8687">{{cite book|title=Celtic – A Century with Honour|last=Wilson|first=Brian|date=1988|publisher=Willow Books|isbn=0-00-218230-0|pages=86–87}}</ref> Results improved in the early 1950s with the [[Coronation Cup (football)|Coronation Cup]] win in 1953 and a league and cup double in 1954.<ref name="celticfc" /> McGrory also led Celtic to a famous 7–1 [[Scottish League Cup]] Final win over [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] in 1957, still a record score in a major British cup final.<ref>{{cite web|last=Sullivan|first=Joe|title=Oh Hampden in the sun|url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/1671|access-date=30 December 2016|publisher=Celtic F.C.|date=19 October 2016|archive-date=29 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201229202443/https://www.celticfc.com/news/1671|url-status=live}}</ref> His time as manager is considered largely a period of underachievement, and with chairman [[Robert Kelly (football chairman)|Robert Kelly]]'s domineering influence in the running of the club, many questioned how much say McGrory had in team selection.<ref name="celticfc" /><ref name="wilson8687" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Players – Bobby Collins – Part 2 – Home grown hero|url=http://www.mightyleeds.co.uk/pdf/playercollinspart2.pdf|website=The Mighty Mighty Whites|access-date=14 February 2014|page=5|archive-date=4 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304100952/http://www.mightyleeds.co.uk/pdf/playercollinspart2.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===1965–1991===
[[File:John "Jock" Stein Bronze statue outside Celtic Parkhead stadium by sculptor John McKenna.jpg|thumb|220px|alt=Statue of Jock Stein holding the European Cup|[[Jock Stein]] was the first British football manager to win the European Cup, leading Celtic to victory over [[Inter Milan]] in 1967.]]
[[File:Mcneill.jpg|thumb|220px|alt=Photograph of Billy McNeill takan in the early 1980s|[[Billy McNeill]] had two spells as manager of Celtic, having also captained the side to their [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] win in 1967 as a player.]]

[[Jock Stein]] left [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] to become manager of Celtic in 1965, with McGrory taking on the role of the club's Public Relations Officer.<ref>{{cite web|last=Jacobs|first=Raymond|title=Mr Stein to become Celtic manager – New post for McGrory|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=4GJAAAAAIBAJ&pg=1782%2C39375|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|date=1 February 1965|access-date=27 January 2018|via=Google|archive-date=15 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210215093435/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=4GJAAAAAIBAJ&pg=1782%2C39375|url-status=live}}</ref> As a player, Stein had previously captained Celtic to their league and cup double success in 1954,<ref>{{cite news|title=Remembering Jock Stein|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/scotland/4221034.stm|access-date=17 September 2015|publisher=BBC Sport|date=6 September 2005|archive-date=2 May 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090502024835/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/scotland/4221034.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> and then as manager led [[Dunfermline Athletic F.C.|Dunfermline]] to a famous [[1961 Scottish Cup Final|cup final]] win over Celtic in 1961.<ref>{{cite news|last=Henderson|first=Gair|title=Heroes one and all|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Gk5BAAAAIBAJ&pg=5952%2C7017478|newspaper=Evening Times|location=Glasgow|date=27 April 1961|page=22|access-date=27 January 2018|via=Google|archive-date=6 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406005809/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Gk5BAAAAIBAJ&pg=5952,7017478|url-status=live}}</ref> On succeeding from McGrory, Stein took full control of all team matters.<ref>{{cite news |last=Rafferty |first=John |title=Jock Stein to have full control over Celtic players |url=https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/teams/celtic/jock-stein-to-have-full-control-over-celtic-players-1-3724628 |newspaper=The Scotsman |location=Edinburgh |date=13 February 1965 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127192030/https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/teams/celtic/jock-stein-to-have-full-control-over-celtic-players-1-3724628 |url-status=live }}</ref> He is considered a football visionary; transforming a side lacking direction and having gone almost eight years without a trophy into the best team in Europe, all whilst playing entertaining adventurous football.<ref name="celticfc" /> In contrast to his predecessors, he was actively involved in his players training – a "tracksuit manager".<ref name="j30">{{cite news |last=Lamont |first=Alasdair |title=Jock Stein's legacy endures 30 years after untimely death |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34212889 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=10 September 2015 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127192158/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/34212889 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last1=Potter|first1=David|last2=Campbell|first2=Tom|title=Jock Stein: The Celtic Years|date=October 1999|publisher=Mainstream Digital|isbn=978-1-84018-241-5|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=1GBb9phYrywC&q=But+Jock+Stein+was+rarely+out+of+it+at+training&pg=PA1936}}</ref> And whereas training had previously consisted of mainly running around the track, he introduced practising with the ball into training.<ref name="j30" /> A Scottish Cup Final win was achieved within weeks of taking up the reins as manager,<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic revive old tradition|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/W0oQwRjWFkrVXTed31xnOA448847|access-date=17 September 2015|newspaper=[[The Times]]|location=London|date=26 April 1965|archive-date=4 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304040631/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/W0oQwRjWFkrVXTed31xnOA448847|url-status=live}}</ref> followed in 1966 by the first of nine consecutive league championship wins.<ref>{{cite news|last=Jacobs|first=Raymond|title=Celtic win League Championship|url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/dbb7028c4a077acd831fae63d91f1ef0/GW456H1584|access-date=17 September 2015|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|date=9 May 1966|pages=9|archive-date=20 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151120100655/http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/3/dbb7028c4a077acd831fae63d91f1ef0/GW456H1584|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Cuddihy|first=Paul|title=Nine-in-a-row was sealed 40 years ago|url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/5768|access-date=17 September 2015|publisher=Celtic F.C.|date=27 April 2014|archive-date=21 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151121154257/http://www.celticfc.net/news/5768|url-status=live}}</ref> In his second full season as manager, Stein led Celtic to success in all five competitions they took part in (a [[List of association football teams to have won four or more trophies in one season#Five titles in one season|"quintuple"]]),<ref name=Brewin>{{cite news |last=Brewin |first=John |title=Greatest Managers, No. 17: Jock Stein |url=http://www.espn.co.uk/football/celtic/story/1514152/greatest-managers-no-17-jock-stein |publisher=ESPN |date=5 August 2013 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127181041/http://www.espn.co.uk/football/celtic/story/1514152/greatest-managers-no-17-jock-stein |url-status=live }}</ref> most memorably their 2–1 win in [[Lisbon]] over [[Inter Milan]] in the [[1967 European Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic win European Cup 1967|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/scotland/sportscotland/asportingnation/article/0045/|website=BBC – A Sporting Nation|access-date=17 September 2015|archive-date=14 May 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160514021536/http://www.bbc.co.uk/scotland/sportscotland/asportingnation/article/0045/|url-status=live}}</ref> He led Celtic to a further European Cup final in 1970, knocking out [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] and [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] en route, but lost to [[Feyenoord]] 2–1 after extra time in the final.<ref>{{cite web|title=Season 1969–70|url=http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|website=European Cup History|access-date=6 October 2013|archive-date=18 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180818204130/http://www.europeancuphistory.com/euro70.html|url-status=usurped}}</ref> He was seriously injured in a car accident in July 1975 and spent the next year recuperating, with assistant manager [[Sean Fallon (footballer)|Sean Fallon]] taking over managerial duties for the season.<ref name="ipost">{{cite news|last=Purden|first=Richard|title=Jock Stein: 30 years on from his death, we reflect on an unsung season|url=http://irishpost.co.uk/jock-stein-30-years-on-since-his-death-we-reflect-on-an-unsung-season/|access-date=17 September 2015|newspaper=The Irish Post|location=London|date=10 September 2015|archive-date=12 September 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150912043622/http://irishpost.co.uk/jock-stein-30-years-on-since-his-death-we-reflect-on-an-unsung-season/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="cw282">{{cite book |title=The Glory & The Dream |date=1987 |publisher=Grafton Books|isbn=0-586-20005-3|last1=Campbell |first1=Tom |last2=Woods |first2=Pat |page=282}}</ref> On his return in season 1976–77, Stein led Celtic to a league and cup double; these would be the last honours he would win at Celtic.<ref name="ipost" /> After winning 25 major trophies,<ref name="celticfc" /> Stein finally relinquished his role in 1978.<ref name="ipost" />

In 1978 former player and captain of the European Cup winning team, [[Billy McNeill]] took over as manager, having spent the previous season managing [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Paul|first=Ian|title=McNeill takes Clark with him to Parkhead|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Ufw9AAAAIBAJ&pg=3128%2C6939134|newspaper=The Glasgow Herald|date=30 May 1978|access-date=27 January 2018|via=Google|archive-date=6 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406005806/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Ufw9AAAAIBAJ&pg=3128,6939134|url-status=live}}</ref> He brought the league title back to Parkhead in his first season, clinching the championship in their final match of the season with a 4–2 win over Rangers.<ref>{{cite news|last=Davidson|first=Alan|title=Incredible! The night passion and courage built a legend|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=TO9AAAAAIBAJ&pg=5536%2C4652616|newspaper=Evening Times|location=Glasgow|date=22 May 1979|page=31|access-date=27 January 2018|via=Google|archive-date=6 April 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406005808/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=TO9AAAAAIBAJ&pg=5536,4652616|url-status=live}}</ref> A Cup Final win in 1980<ref>{{cite book|title=The Glory & The Dream|last1=Campbell|last2=Woods|first1=Tom|first2=Pat|date=1987|pages=302–303|publisher=Grafton Books|isbn=0-586-20005-3}}</ref> was followed by back-to-back titles in 1981 and 1982.<ref name="gone">{{cite web |last=Craig |first=Jim |url=http://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2012/06/30/if-you-know-the-history-30th-june-1983-billy-mcneill-heads-for-manchester-city/ |title=If You Know The History 30th June 1983 Billy McNeill heads for Manchester City |website=Jim Craig Celtic Supporter's Club |date=30 June 2012 |access-date=5 August 2013 |archive-date=11 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150611200609/https://jimcraigcsc.wordpress.com/2012/06/30/if-you-know-the-history-30th-june-1983-billy-mcneill-heads-for-manchester-city/ |url-status=live }}</ref> A public row however with the board over a contract and funds for new players resulted in McNeill leaving the club in 1983.<ref name="gone" /><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BOBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3124,696414&dq=celtic+directors+money+stein&hl=en |title=Billy and the Board |newspaper=Evening Times |location=Glasgow |date=4 November 1986 |access-date=27 January 2018 |via=Google |archive-date=15 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210215093508/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BOBAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3124%2C696414&dq=celtic+directors+money+stein&hl=en |url-status=live }}</ref> At only 35 years old, [[David Hay]] succeeded McNeill.<ref name="celticfc" /> A frustrating first season saw Celtic finish runners-up in each of the domestic competitions.<ref name="celticfc" /> Success did eventually arrive though, winning the centenary [[1985 Scottish Cup Final|Scottish Cup]] in 1985<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19850520&id=V7ZAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2438,4527776 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=20 May 1985 |page=17 |title=Roy of Celtic marches forward for Cup glory |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |access-date=27 January 2018 |via=Google |archive-date=6 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406005807/https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19850520&id=V7ZAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2438,4527776 |url-status=live }}</ref> and then a famous last day championship win at [[Love Street (stadium)|Love Street]] in 1986, defeating [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]] 5–0 whilst title rivals [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] lost at [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]], securing the league on [[goal difference]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19860505&id=l7ZAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2512,1047906 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=5 May 1986 |page=10 |title=Celtic keep bargain – with a little help from Dundee |first=Ian |last=Paul |access-date=27 January 2018 |via=Google |archive-date=29 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201229202530/https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19860505&id=l7ZAAAAAIBAJ&pg=2512%2C1047906 |url-status=live }}</ref> Hay was unable to cope with high spending Rangers the following year, and on failing to win any trophies was sacked by the club.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://programmes.stv.tv/the-football-years/1986/the-full-story/227598-the-full-story-souness-rangers-revolution-terry-butcher/ |title=The Full Story: Souness' Rangers Revolution – Terry Butcher – The Full Story |publisher=STV |date=4 March 2011 |access-date=5 August 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131213015952/http://programmes.stv.tv/the-football-years/1986/the-full-story/227598-the-full-story-souness-rangers-revolution-terry-butcher/ |archive-date=13 December 2013 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="return">{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=6yo1AAAAIBAJ&pg=2834%2C7639288 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=29 May 1987 |page=13 |title='Nice guy' Hay makes way for Caesar |first=Ian |last=Paul |access-date=27 January 2018 |via=Google |archive-date=6 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230406005807/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=6yo1AAAAIBAJ&pg=2834,7639288 |url-status=live }}</ref> Billy McNeill dramatically returned to the club in the summer of 1987<ref name="return" /> and went on to lead the club to a league and cup double in its [[1987–88 Celtic F.C. season|centenary season of 1987–88]].<ref>{{cite book|title=Celtic – A Century with Honour|last=Wilson|first=Brian|date=1988|publisher=Willow Books|isbn=0-00-218230-0|page=189}}</ref> A further Cup Final win followed in 1989,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19890522&id=izNAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3687,251027 |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=22 May 1989 |page=22 |title=Tension and fear spoil show-piece |first=James |last=Traynor |access-date=27 January 2018 |via=Google |archive-date=11 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211211060301/https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=2507&dat=19890522&id=izNAAAAAIBAJ&pg=3687%2C251027 |url-status=live }}</ref> but the club went into a dramatic decline after that.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/11943955.Stagnant_Celtic_pay_ultimate_penalty__Snelders_saves_Rogan_apos_s_kick_and_Irvine_secures_cup_for_Aberdeen/ |title=Stagnant Celtic pay ultimate penalty. Snelders saves Rogan's kick and Irvine secures cup for Aberdeen |newspaper=The Glasgow Herald |date=14 May 1990 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127200242/http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/11943955.Stagnant_Celtic_pay_ultimate_penalty__Snelders_saves_Rogan_apos_s_kick_and_Irvine_secures_cup_for_Aberdeen/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="bmsack">{{cite news |title=A sacking out of the bag |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12686178.A_sacking_out_of_the_bag/ |newspaper=The Herald |location=Glasgow |date=6 September 1994 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127200325/http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12686178.A_sacking_out_of_the_bag/ |url-status=live }}</ref> After two seasons without any honours, McNeill was sacked in 1991.<ref name="bmsack" />

===1991–2000===
Former [[Republic of Ireland national football team|Republic of Ireland]] international, [[Liam Brady]], became the first Celtic manager who had never previously played for the club.<ref name="celticfc" /> He failed to win any trophies in his first two seasons or reach any cup finals, and with no progress apparent into this third season he resigned in October 1993.<ref name="celticfc" /> [[Frank Connor]] took interim charge for several matches,<ref>{{cite news|title=A frank Connor knows the score with Celtic|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12714744.A_frank_Connor_knows_the_score_with_Celtic/|access-date=16 September 2015|newspaper=The Herald|location=Glasgow|date=22 October 1993|archive-date=21 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151121083044/http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12714744.A_frank_Connor_knows_the_score_with_Celtic/|url-status=live}}</ref> before [[Lou Macari]] was appointed manager.<ref name="celticfc" /> Despite defeating Rangers 2–1 at Ibrox in his first match, results otherwise failed to improve.<ref name="celticfc" /> [[Fergus McCann]] took over as owner of Celtic in March 1994 and duly sacked Macari three months later.<ref name="celticfc" /> Following Macari's sacking, [[Tommy Burns (footballer)|Tommy Burns]] was appointed manager in the summer of 1994.<ref>{{cite news |title=As Celtic doors reopen, another looks set to close. Burns' continuing reshuffle now leaves Grimes in limbo |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12735451.As_Celtic_doors_reopen__another_looks_set_to_close__Burns_apos__continuing_reshuffle_now_leaves_Grimes_in_limbo/ |newspaper=The Herald |location=Glasgow |date=23 July 1994 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=30 May 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170530230908/http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12735451.As_Celtic_doors_reopen__another_looks_set_to_close__Burns_apos__continuing_reshuffle_now_leaves_Grimes_in_limbo/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic won their first trophy in six years at the end of the season, beating [[Airdrieonians F.C. (1878)|Airdrie]] 1–0 in the Scottish Cup final.<ref name="celticfc" /> However, this was an era of domestic dominance by Rangers and despite an outstanding second season in 1995–96 where only one league match was lost, Burns could still finish only second in the league behind the Ibrox club.<ref name="celticfc" /> Despite expensive players arriving at Parkhead,<ref>{{cite news |first=Hugh |last=Keevins |title=McCann: I had to run 'Three Amigos' out of town |url=https://www.thefreelibrary.com/McCANN%3A+I+HAD+TO+RUN+THREE+AMIGOS+OUT+OF+TOWN.-a060751699 |newspaper=Sunday Mail |location=London |date=1 November 1998 |access-date=27 January 2018 |via=TheFreeLibrary.com |archive-date=28 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180128132709/https://www.thefreelibrary.com/McCANN%3A+I+HAD+TO+RUN+THREE+AMIGOS+OUT+OF+TOWN.-a060751699 |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic remained unable to overhaul Rangers the following season in 1997.<ref name="celticfc" /> After losing to [[Falkirk F.C.|Falkirk]] in the semi-final of the Scottish Cup, Burns was sacked and his assistant [[Billy Stark]] took charge for the few remaining games of the season.<ref>{{cite news |title=Burns is finally out The 'amicable' parting is not appreciated by more than 2000 angry fans outside Parkhead as even the manager has a police escort to leave |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12323298.Burns_is_finally_out_The__apos_amicable_apos__parting_is_not_appreciated_by_more_than_2000_angry_fans_____________outside_Parkhead_as_even_the_manager_has_a_police_escort_to_leave/ |newspaper=The Herald |location=Glasgow |date=3 May 1997 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=28 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180128074745/http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12323298.Burns_is_finally_out_The__apos_amicable_apos__parting_is_not_appreciated_by_more_than_2000_angry_fans_____________outside_Parkhead_as_even_the_manager_has_a_police_escort_to_leave/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the summer of 1997, former European Cup winner and Dutch international, [[Wim Jansen]], became head coach.<ref name="CFCBriefHistory" /> Despite losing his first two games of the season,<ref>{{cite news |last=McKinney |first=David |title=Jansen left searching for clues |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/jansen-left-searching-for-clues-1246019.html |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=17 August 1997 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127223840/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/jansen-left-searching-for-clues-1246019.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Jansen quickly turned things round and won the club's first Scottish League Cup in 15 years when they beat [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] 3–0 in the final.<ref name="celticfc" /> A tightly fought league campaign saw Celtic clinch the title on the final day of the season, stopping Rangers' bid for 10 league titles in a row.<ref name="celticfc" /> However it had by then become known that Jansen was disgruntled with Celtic managing director, [[Jock Brown]], and he quit in the immediate aftermath of Celtic's title win.<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic fans cry foul as Jansen quits|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12296943.Celtic_fans_cry_foul_as_Jansen_quits/|access-date=16 September 2015|newspaper=The Herald|location=Glasgow|date=12 May 1998|archive-date=21 November 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151121084734/http://www.heraldscotland.com/news/12296943.Celtic_fans_cry_foul_as_Jansen_quits/|url-status=live}}</ref> Veteran Slovak coach [[Jozef Vengloš]] arrived at Celtic for the following season. A poor start to the season put Vengloš under pressure, but the arrival of new signings and a 5–1 win over Rangers in the league gave rise to optimism. However the league deficit was too much to claw back and a Scottish Cup final defeat to Rangers sealed a largely disappointing season.<ref name="celticfc" /> The summer of 1999 saw the arrival of [[Kenny Dalglish]] as [[director of football]] and [[John Barnes (footballer)|John Barnes]] as head coach.<ref name="celticfc" /> A bright start soon faded as Celtic began to drop vital points in the league.<ref name="celticfc" /> A shock Cup defeat at home to [[Inverness Caledonian Thistle F.C.|Inverness Caledonian Thistle]] saw Barnes sacked and Dalglish taking over as [[caretaker manager]] for the rest of the season.<ref name="celticfc" /> Celtic did win the League Cup,<ref name="celticfc" /> but slumped in the league and finished 21 points behind winners Rangers.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.statto.com/football/stats/scotland/premier-league/1999-2000/table |title=Scottish Premier League 1999–2000 Table |publisher=Statto Organisation |access-date=6 August 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112190945/http://www.statto.com/football/stats/scotland/premier-league/1999-2000/table |archive-date=12 November 2013 |url-status=live}}</ref>

===2000–2019===
[[File:O'Neill, Martin.jpg|thumb|220px|alt=Photograph of Martin O'Neill|[[Martin O'Neill]] won the domestic treble in his first season as manager and led Celtic to the UEFA Cup Final in 2003, their first European final in over 30 years.]]

[[Martin O'Neill]], a former [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] winner as a player with [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]], took charge of the club in June 2000.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2000/jun/01/newsstory.sport9 |title=O'Neill confirmed as Celtic manager |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=1 June 2000 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=31 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161231080230/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2000/jun/01/newsstory.sport9 |url-status=live }}</ref> O'Neill's first [[Old Firm]] game, in late August 2000, ended in a 6–2 victory for Celtic.<ref name = "top ten"/> It was their biggest victory over Rangers since the [[1957 Scottish League Cup Final]]. His second Old Firm game saw a reversal of fortunes, however, as Celtic suffered a 5–1 defeat.<ref name="top ten">{{cite web |url=http://www.skysports.com/interactive/top_tens_story/0,25722,15881_5595879,00.html |website=Sky Sports |title=Topical Top Tens |access-date=22 June 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140116071459/http://www.skysports.com/interactive/top_tens_story/0%2C25722%2C15881_5595879%2C00.html |archive-date=16 January 2014}}</ref> In that first season, O'Neill won a domestic treble with Celtic, the first time this had been achieved since 1968–69. He was then touted as a potential successor to [[Alex Ferguson]], who had announced he was to leave [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in 2002.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/1340732.stm |title=Ferguson touts O'Neill as successor |date=20 May 2001 |access-date=13 June 2015 |work=BBC Sport}}</ref> Celtic then retained the league title in 2001–02, the first time since 1982 that Celtic had managed that feat. Celtic also qualified for the Champions League group stage, winning all of their home games but losing all of their away games.{{citation needed|date=November 2025}}

He then guided Celtic to the [[2003 UEFA Cup Final]] in [[Seville]], which Celtic lost 3–2 in extra time to a [[FC Porto|Porto]] side managed by [[José Mourinho]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Porto end Celtic's Uefa dream |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/3043029.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=21 May 2003 |access-date=23 April 2012}}</ref> This was Celtic's first European final since 1970 and they beat Blackburn, Celta Vigo, Stuttgart, Liverpool and Boavista on the way to the final. He was named on the five-man shortlist for [[UEFA Team of the Year]] in the manager category in 2003
<ref>{{cite news |title=uefa.com Team of the Year 2003 nominations |url=https://www.uefa.com/newsfiles/133586.pdf |publisher=UEFA |access-date=31 January 2021}}</ref> The following season Celtic regained the league title from rivals Rangers and reached the quarter-finals of the UEFA Cup, with their run seeing them knock out Barcelona.{{citation needed|date=November 2025}}

On 25 May 2005, Celtic announced that O'Neill would resign as manager to care for his wife, Geraldine, who had [[lymphoma]]. His last competitive game in charge of Celtic was the Scottish Cup final 1–0 victory over [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]] on 28 May 2005, decided by an eleventh-minute goal by [[Alan Thompson (footballer, born 1973)|Alan Thompson]].<ref>{{cite news |title=O'Neill leaves Bhoys with Cup win |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_div_1/4589807.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=28 May 2005 |access-date=23 April 2012}}</ref>

Under O'Neill, Celtic won 213, drew 29 and lost 40 of 282 games played, making him the most successful Celtic manager since Jock Stein. In his five seasons at Celtic Park, O'Neill won three Scottish Premier League titles, three Scottish Cups, and a League Cup. The two league titles he lost were by margins of a goal and a point, respectively. He also oversaw a record seven consecutive victories in Old Firm derbies, and in the 2003–04 season Celtic created a British record of 25 consecutive league victories.<ref name=25inarow>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/mar/11/manchester-united-consecutive-league-wins-the-knowledge |title=Have Manchester United just set a record for consecutive league wins? |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=11 March 2009|access-date=14 January 2011 |first1=Rob |last1=Smyth |first2=John |last2=Ashdown}}</ref> His win rate of 75.5% is the highest of any manager in the club's history.<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic Manager Records |url=https://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/manager_records.php |publisher=fitba stats |access-date=31 January 2021}}</ref>

[[Gordon Strachan]] was announced as O'Neill's replacement in June 2005 and after winning the SPL title in his first year in charge,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/scottish/celtic-1-heart-of-midlothian-0-strachans-joy-as-celtic-are-crowned-champions-473001.html |title=Celtic 1 Heart of Midlothian 0: Strachan's joy as Celtic are crowned champions |first=Nick |last=Harris |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=6 April 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150904075246/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/scottish/celtic-1-heart-of-midlothian-0-strachans-joy-as-celtic-are-crowned-champions-473001.html |archive-date=4 September 2015 |url-status=dead}}</ref> he went on to become only the third Celtic manager to win three titles in a row. He also guided Celtic to their first [[UEFA Champions League]] knockout stage in [[2006–07 UEFA Champions League|2006–07]]<ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/6160910.stm |title=Celtic 1–0 Man Utd |first=Colin |last=Moffat |publisher=BBC Sport |date=21 November 2006 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=10 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070910195624/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/6160910.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> and repeated the feat in [[UEFA Champions League 2007-08|2007–08]]<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/sport/football/european/milan-1-celtic-0-inzaghi-delight-as-celtic-defeat-turns-into-celebration-28070020.html |title=Milan 1 Celtic 0: Inzaghi delight as Celtic defeat turns into celebration |last=Tynan |first=Gordon |newspaper=Belfast Telegraph |date=5 December 2007 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=28 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180128132724/https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/sport/football/european/milan-1-celtic-0-inzaghi-delight-as-celtic-defeat-turns-into-celebration-28070020.html |url-status=live }}</ref> before departing the club in May 2009, after failing to win the [[2008–09 Scottish Premier League|SPL title]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/5383302/Gordon-Strachan-stands-down-at-Celtic.html |title=Gordon Strachan stands down at Celtic |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=25 May 2009 |last=Forsyth |first=Roddy |access-date=4 April 2018 |archive-date=22 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181122120918/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/5383302/Gordon-Strachan-stands-down-at-Celtic.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Tony Mowbray]] took charge of the club in June 2009,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/jun/16/tony-mowbray-celtic-west-bromwich |title=Tony Mowbray confirmed as new manager of Celtic |last=Murray |first=Ewan |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=16 June 2009 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=31 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161231080207/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/jun/16/tony-mowbray-celtic-west-bromwich |url-status=live }}</ref> and he was succeeded a year later by [[Neil Lennon]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e6-0e74b03bfd58-1bd55f42ec53-1000--lennon-the-way-forward-for-celtic/ |title=Lennon the way forward for Celtic |publisher=UEFA |date=9 June 2010 |access-date=16 September 2015 |archive-date=2 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160102110950/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/newsid%3D1496724.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic narrowly lost out to Rangers in the league in Lennon's first season in charge, but he did gain consolation by defeating Motherwell 3–0 in the [[2011 Scottish Cup Final]]. Lennon went on to win three league titles in a row, then announced his departure from the club in May 2014 after four years in the role.<ref>{{cite news |last=McLaughlin |first=Chris |title=Celtic: Neil Lennon exits but will help find successor |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27519596 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=22 May 2014 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127234635/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27519596 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Norwegian [[Ronny Deila]] was appointed manager of Celtic in June 2014.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27718845 |title=Celtic: Ronny Deila named new manager of Scottish champions |publisher=BBC Sport |date=6 June 2014 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127234009/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27718845 |url-status=live }}</ref> He went on to lead Celtic to two consecutive league titles and a League Cup, but the team's performances in European competition were poor. Following being knocked out of the Scottish Cup by Rangers in April 2016, Deila announced he would leave the club at the end of the season.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ronny Deila admits 'disappointments' in announcing Celtic resignation |url=https://www.thenationalnews.com/sport/ronny-deila-admits-disappointments-in-announcing-celtic-resignation-1.146334 |newspaper=The National |location=Abu Dhabi |date=20 April 2016 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180127225355/https://www.thenational.ae/sport/ronny-deila-admits-disappointments-in-announcing-celtic-resignation-1.146334 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="brendan">{{cite news |last=Murray |first=Ewan |title=Celtic appoint Brendan Rodgers as manager to take over from Ronny Deila |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/may/20/celtic-appoint-brendan-rodgers-manager |newspaper=The Guardian |date=20 May 2016 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=20 May 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160520184709/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/may/20/celtic-appoint-brendan-rodgers-manager |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Cel-Zen (12) (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|180px|alt=Photograph of Brendan Rodgers|[[Brendan Rodgers]] managed Celtic to two consecutive [[domestic treble]]s.]]In May 2016, [[Brendan Rodgers]] was announced as Deila's successor.<ref name="brendan" /><ref>{{cite news|last=Kane|first=Desmond|title=Brendan Rodgers finds his Paradise: Why Glasgow Celtic remain one of world's great clubs|url=http://www.eurosport.co.uk/football/scottish-premiership/2015-2016/brendan-rodgers-discovers-his-paradise-why-glasgow-celtic-remain-one-of-world-s-truly-great-clubs_sto5579989/story.shtml|access-date=23 May 2016|publisher=Eurosport|date=21 May 2016|archive-date=10 June 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160610032058/http://www.eurosport.co.uk/football/scottish-premiership/2015-2016/brendan-rodgers-discovers-his-paradise-why-glasgow-celtic-remain-one-of-world-s-truly-great-clubs_sto5579989/story.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref> In his first season the club won their 100th major trophy, defeating Aberdeen in the League Cup Final in November 2016,<ref>{{cite news|title=League Cup final: Aberdeen 0–3 Celtic as it happened|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/scotland/38121946|access-date=1 December 2016|publisher=BBC Sport|date=27 November 2016|archive-date=29 November 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161129092819/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/scotland/38121946|url-status=live}}</ref> and clinched the league title in April 2017 with a record eight league games to spare.<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic's title triumph by numbers|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39330673|access-date=4 April 2017|publisher=BBC Sport|date=2 April 2017|archive-date=4 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170404164547/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39330673|url-status=live}}</ref> Celtic finished their league campaign undefeated, the first time a [[Scottish football league system|Scottish top-flight]] side had done so since 1899.<ref>{{cite news|last=Campbell|first=Andy|title=Celtic 2–0 Hearts|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39911911|access-date=22 May 2017|publisher=BBC Sport|date=21 May 2017|archive-date=22 May 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170522000052/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39911911|url-status=live}}</ref> In May 2017, Celtic defeated Aberdeen 2–1 in the Scottish Cup final, clinching the club's fourth domestic treble, as well as securing an unprecedented domestic season unbeaten.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39984102 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=27 May 2017 |access-date=28 May 2017 |first=Tom |last=English |title=Celtic 2–1 Aberdeen |archive-date=11 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191011193111/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39984102 |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic continued their unbeaten domestic run into the following season, eventually extending it to 69 games, surpassing their own 100-year-old British record of 62 games set by [[Willie Maley]], before finally losing to Hearts.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/41870987|title=Celtic: Incredible feat to beat 100-year-old British record – Brendan Rodgers|date=4 November 2017|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=7 November 2017|archive-date=7 November 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171107053019/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/41870987|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=McLauchlin|first=Brian|title=Heart of Midlothian 4–0 Celtic|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42297312|access-date=17 January 2018|publisher=BBC Sport|date=17 December 2017|archive-date=29 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180429025740/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/42297312|url-status=live}}</ref> In November 2017, Celtic defeated [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] in the [[2017 Scottish League Cup Final]],<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/nov/26/celtic-motherwell-scottish-league-cup-final-match-report |title=Forrest and Dembélé seal Scottish League Cup for Celtic over Motherwell |last=Murray |first=Ewan |newspaper=The Guardian |date=26 November 2017 |access-date=27 January 2018 |archive-date=27 November 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171127205432/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/nov/26/celtic-motherwell-scottish-league-cup-final-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> before a seventh consecutive league title was clinched in April 2018,<ref>{{cite news|last=Murray|first=Ewan|title=Celtic seal Scottish Premiership title with 5-0 rampage over Rangers|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/apr/29/celtic-rangers-scottish-premiership-match-report|access-date=20 May 2018|work=The Guardian|date=29 April 2018|archive-date=21 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180521191437/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/apr/29/celtic-rangers-scottish-premiership-match-report|url-status=live}}</ref> with Celtic then going on to defeat Motherwell in the [[2018 Scottish Cup Final]] to clinch a second consecutive domestic treble (the "double treble"), the first club in Scotland to do so.<ref>{{cite news|last=Forsyth|first=Roddy|title=Celtic claim unprecedented double treble with comfortable Scottish Cup final win over Motherwell|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/05/19/celtic-claim-unprecedented-double-treble-comfortable-scottish/|access-date=20 May 2018|work=The Telegraph|date=19 May 2018|archive-date=19 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180619031118/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2018/05/19/celtic-claim-unprecedented-double-treble-comfortable-scottish/|url-status=live}}</ref> Into his third season, Rodgers led Celtic to another League Cup, defeating Aberdeen in the final, and by February 2019 the club were eight points clear in the league. However, on 26 February 2019 Rodgers left Celtic in a surprise move to take over as manager of [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]].<ref>{{cite news |last=McLaughlin |first=Chris |title=Brendan Rodgers: Celtic boss set to join Leicester after permission for talks |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47368961 |access-date=27 February 2019 |work=BBC Sport |date=26 February 2019 |archive-date=27 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190227020309/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47368961 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Brendan Rodgers: Leicester City appoint former Celtic boss as manager |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47368712 |access-date=27 February 2019 |work=BBC Sport |date=26 February 2019 |archive-date=26 February 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190226190303/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/47368712 |url-status=live }}</ref> Neil Lennon returned to Celtic to work as caretaker manager until the end of the season. He went on to help Celtic complete their eighth successive league title, and then to a 2–1 win over [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] in the [[2019 Scottish Cup Final]] to clinch the treble for the third season in a row (the "treble treble").<ref>{{cite news |last=Lindsay |first=Clive |title=Relive the drama as Celtic beat Hearts to clinch treble treble at Hampden |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/48339050 |access-date=7 June 2019 |work=BBC Sport |date=25 May 2019 |archive-date=25 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190525175800/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/48339050 |url-status=live }}</ref> The following week, Lennon was confirmed as full-time manager for the second time.<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic appoint Neil Lennon as manager for second time |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48472791 |access-date=7 June 2019 |work=BBC Sport |date=31 May 2019 |archive-date=31 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190531144931/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/48472791 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===2019–present===
{{Update|section|date=November 2025}}
Into the first full season of his second spell as manager, [[Neil Lennon]] led [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] to a 1–0 win over [[Rangers F.C|Rangers]] in the [[2019 Scottish League Cup Final]], the club's tenth consecutive domestic trophy.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Forsyth |first1=Roddy |last2=Bagchi |first2=Rob |title=Celtic make it 10 trophies in a row after magnificent Fraser Forster frustrates Rangers |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/12/08/rangers-vs-celtic-scottish-league-cup-final-live-score-latest/ |access-date=10 December 2019 |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |date=8 December 2019 |archive-date=10 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191210002421/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2019/12/08/rangers-vs-celtic-scottish-league-cup-final-live-score-latest/ |url-status=live }}</ref> By March 2020, Celtic were 13 points clear in the league and well on the way to a ninth consecutive title: However, all professional football in Scotland was suspended later that month due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United Kingdom]], resulting in demands from the [[news media]] in Scotland for the league season to be declared "null and void".<ref>{{cite news |title=Coronavirus in Scotland: Which Scottish events have been cancelled due to COVID-19? |url=https://www.heraldscotland.com/news/18304715.coronavirus-scotland-scottish-events-cancelled-due-covid-19/ |access-date=16 March 2020 |work=Herald Scotland |date=14 March 2020 |archive-date=1 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200501085734/https://www.heraldscotland.com/news/18304715.coronavirus-scotland-scottish-events-cancelled-due-covid-19/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=McDermott |first=Scott |title=Celtic and Rangers title spat shows SPFL must consider the null and void elephant in the room |url=https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-rangers-title-spat-shows-21852483 |access-date=13 April 2020 |work=Daily Record |date=12 April 2020 |archive-date=13 April 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200413003324/https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/celtic-rangers-title-spat-shows-21852483 |url-status=live }}</ref> However, after several weeks of controversy, Celtic were confirmed as champions on 18 May 2020 following a league board meeting the previous week where it was agreed that completing the full league campaign was unfeasible.<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic champions & Hearts relegated after SPFL ends season |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/52646282 |access-date=20 May 2020 |work=BBC Sport |date=18 May 2020 |archive-date=18 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200518114202/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/52646282 |url-status=live }}</ref> The completion of the [[2019-20 Scottish Cup]] was delayed due to the ongoing COVID-19 pandemic, with the semi-finals and final not taking place until late autumn/ winter of 2020. Celtic and [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] reached the final that took place on 20 December 2020. The match went to [[Penalty shootout|penalty-kicks]] after the sides [[Tie (draw)|tied at 3–3]] after [[extra time]], with Celtic going on to win the penalty shoot-out to clinch a fourth successive treble.<ref name="hct1920">{{cite news |last=Lindsay |first=Clive |title=Celtic 3 - 3 Heart of Midlothian |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55291058 |access-date=22 December 2020 |work=BBC Sport |date=20 December 2020 |archive-date=19 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201219195302/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55291058 |url-status=live }}</ref> This win saw Lennon become the first to win a treble as both player and manager.<ref name="hct1920" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic: Neil Lennon thanks board as he becomes first to win treble as player and manager |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55388016 |access-date=28 June 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=20 December 2020 |archive-date=22 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201222005950/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55388016 |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic struggled throughout [[2020–21 in Scottish football|season 2020–21]] though; poor performances in Europe, knocked out of the League Cup by [[Ross County F.C.|Ross County]] (bringing to an end a run of 35 domestic cup wins), and by February 2021 were trailing 18 points behind Rangers in the league - effectively ending Celtic's hopes of "ten in a row" league titles. Lennon resigned on 24 February 2021, with assistant manager [[John Kennedy (Scottish footballer)|John Kennedy]] taking interim charge of the team.<ref>{{cite news |title=Neil Lennon: Celtic manager resigns with side 18 points adrift of Rangers |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55130253 |access-date=27 February 2021 |work=BBC Sport |date=24 February 2021 |archive-date=24 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210224095027/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/55130253 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 10 June 2021, [[Ange Postecoglou]] was announced as the new manager of Celtic.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56634729 |title=Celtic appoint Ange Postecoglou as new manager |website=BBC Sport |date=10 June 2021 |access-date=10 June 2021 |archive-date=10 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210610080532/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/56634729 |url-status=live }}</ref> His first couple of months as manager was a period of upheaval, with 12 first-team players leaving during the summer transfer window while 10 new players were signed.<ref name="foxang">{{cite news |last=Rayson |first=Zac |title=How 'fish out of water' Aussie Ange Postecoglou turned Celtic from basket-case into believers |url=https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/how-fish-out-of-water-aussie-ange-postecoglou-turned-celtic-from-basketcase-into-believers/news-story/ef747e271372277890407a7329ab0b8c |access-date=7 February 2022 |work=Fox Sports (Australia) |date=24 November 2021 |archive-date=7 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220207233342/https://www.foxsports.com.au/football/how-fish-out-of-water-aussie-ange-postecoglou-turned-celtic-from-basketcase-into-believers/news-story/ef747e271372277890407a7329ab0b8c |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic lost three of their opening six league matches,<ref>{{cite news |title=Celtic boss Ange Postecoglou remains adamant he has the backing of the fans despite turbulent start |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11787/12414504/celtic-boss-ange-postecoglou-remains-adamant-he-has-the-backing-of-the-fans-despite-turbulent-start |access-date=7 February 2022 |work=Sky Sports |issue=22 September 2021 |archive-date=7 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220207234844/https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11787/12414504/celtic-boss-ange-postecoglou-remains-adamant-he-has-the-backing-of-the-fans-despite-turbulent-start |url-status=live }}</ref> but enjoyed high-scoring wins over [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]] and [[St Mirren F.C.|St Mirren]].<ref name="foxang" /> Improved form in October saw Postecoglou win the [[List of Scottish Professional Football League monthly award winners|Manager of the Month award]],<ref>{{cite news |last=Thomas |first=Josh |title=Ange Postecoglou picks up managerial award as Celtic thrive during 'critical' period |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/au/football/news/ange-postecoglou-picks-up-managerial-award-as-celtic-thrive-during-critical-period/1sl9y1t6d9but12q6en5hq8ca4 |access-date=7 February 2022 |work=Sporting News |date=10 November 2021 |archive-date=7 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220207233343/https://www.sportingnews.com/au/football/news/ange-postecoglou-picks-up-managerial-award-as-celtic-thrive-during-critical-period/1sl9y1t6d9but12q6en5hq8ca4 |url-status=live }}</ref> with new signings [[Jota (footballer, born March 1999)|Jota]] and [[Kyogo Furuhashi]] flourishing in the side.<ref name="foxang" /> In December, Celtic won the first domestic trophy of the season, defeating [[Hibernian F.C|Hibernian]] 2–1 in the [[Scottish League Cup|League Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Duncan |first=Thomas |title=Hibernian 1 - Celtic 2 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/59705941 |access-date=7 February 2022 |work=BBC Sport |date=19 December 2021 |archive-date=28 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220528071130/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/59705941 |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic then went on a run of 31 domestic league games undefeated to regain the league championship at the first attempt. The title was secured following a 1–1 draw with Dundee United at Tannadice on 11 May 2022.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/61316038 |title=Celtic clinch league title after draw at Dundee Utd |work=BBC Sport |access-date=26 May 2022 |archive-date=22 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220922081133/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/61316038 |url-status=live }}</ref> Celtic made a strong start to the following season, and by New Year (2023) were nine points clear of second-placed Rangers in the league, with a significantly higher goal difference.<ref>{{cite news |first=Tom |last=English |work=BBC Sport |access-date=6 June 2023 |date=2 January 2023 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64092615 |title=Rangers 2-2 Celtic |archive-date=4 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230304215434/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64092615 |url-status=live }}</ref> The League Cup was retained with a 2–1 win over Rangers in the final on 26 February 2023, Kyogo scoring twice for Celtic.<ref>{{cite news |first=Tom |last=English |work=BBC Sport |access-date=6 June 2023 |date=26 February 2023 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64688959 |title=Viaplay Cup Final: Rangers 1-2 Celtic |archive-date=2 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230402132659/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/64688959 |url-status=live }}</ref> Back-to-back league titles were clinched on 7 May 2023 with four games to spare after a 2–0 win away at Hearts.<ref>{{cite news |first=Martin |last=Watt |work=BBC Sport |access-date=6 June 2023 |date=7 May 2023 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/65439690 |title=Hearts 0-2 Celtic |archive-date=7 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230507153508/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/65439690 |url-status=live }}</ref> A clean sweep of all domestic trophies for season 2022-23 was completed on 3 June 2023 with Celtic defeating Inverness Caledonian Thistle 3–1 in the Scottish Cup Final, the club's eighth domestic treble (a World Record).<ref>{{cite news |work=Daily Record |date=3 June 2023 |title=Ange Postecoglou admits he was Celtic 'joke' as sceptics fired reminder after Scottish Cup victory seals Treble |access-date=6 June 2023 |url=https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/ange-postecoglou-admits-celtic-joke-30149347 |first=Scott |last=McDermott |archive-date=6 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230606105407/https://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/ange-postecoglou-admits-celtic-joke-30149347 |url-status=live }}</ref>

